          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Lorck directly presents his love for Constance as a true and serious emotional state. His insistence that it is more serious than even he had understood strengthens the avowal's truth-presenting force, independently of whether it is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal opens a single integrated offer in which Lorck gives Constance himself, his life and his soul and asks her to accept. This makes the occurrence an affirmation of broad personal and relational devotion, not merely a report of feeling. The undertaking is substantial but metaphorically and practically underspecified, and Constance immediately refuses it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"nNej, uf nej,* sa hun, og veg uvilkaarlig\\ntilbage.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Although emotionally intense, the expression is a structured avowal followed by a deliberate offer and request for acceptance. The supplied scene does not present the words as a reflex-like or non-propositional affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual core force is needed: the declaration is adequately represented as an avowal of love combined, through its same-turn context, with an offer of relational devotion. Its appeal for acceptance is part of that undertaking and uptake structure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lorck confesses love directly to Constance after she has resisted his advances and asked him to stop. He solemnly reiterates the avowal, couples it with an offer of his whole self, life and soul, and asks for acceptance. Constance refuses and retreats; Lorck then becomes accusatory and coercive.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal avowal is integrated with Lorck's same-turn offer to give Constance himself, his life and his soul. It therefore affirms a broad bond of personal devotion and solicits a reciprocal relationship, though its duration and concrete obligations are unspecified. Constance's “Nej, uf nej” is a direct refusal, so the offered undertaking receives no acceptance.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied evidence explicitly identifies the work as Amalie Skram's Constance Ring.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supplies strong evidence that Lorck is affectively agitated and represents himself as seriously in love, but it cannot conclusively establish his sincerity or define the practical terms and duration of the offered devotion. His threatening and coercive conduct complicates the quality of the professed love without eliminating the utterance's avowal or undertaking force.\"}"
        }
      ],
